are you planning on supporting the  prime-minister steel next week well  let's see what what happens now I think  the good thing about what happened  yesterday is first of all we've got that  final insurance against No Deal and  we've also now going to see this  proposal go straight into bill committee  stage where we can test out and you know  have our final chance to shape brexit  over the coming days because I think the  other thing that was clear about  yesterday is that this deal is now  likely to pass so the coming days the  cut the coming couple of weeks really  are our final chance to shape brexit to  lay down different amendments there'll  be some colleagues who want to lay down  an amendment for a second referendum  others will want to look at that future  relationship and try and shake that  others will want to lay amendments  toughening up the workers rights and  environmental protections that my  colleagues Caroline Flint and Melanie on  got into this agreement so let's see  what comes out the other side now and  that's what we've got to do over the  next few days so notice she's slightly  sidestep the question about whether or  not you're going to be voting for the  deal when it comes back is that because  you're still making your mind up well as  I say I mean let's let's see how things  shape out I mean I probably wouldn't  have voted for it if we'd had a straight  up-and-down vote on it  Oh over the weekend but I do think now  that we've got to move forward we do  have to resolve this issue we have to  resolve brexit even if you're somebody  who is advocating a second referendum  you still need to say what's going to be  on the ballot paper we still need to  have a brexit proposition time is  running out and that might that's why we  we've got to resolve brexit and I've  always thought I think it's been pretty  clear actually that the matters in  Parliament have always been clear that  people didn't want No Deal but the maths  in Parliament have also been there for a  deal for the right kind of deal I would  have preferred a different sort of deal  I would have preferred a much softer  brexit but I'm afraid those  opportunities to shape that brexit worth  watered in previous occasions in the  last few months we had a set of  indicative votes on on lots of versions  of Brack seats we had cross party talks  and unfortunately those on the extremes  of this debate on both sides have  thwarted those efforts so now we're kind  of left with you know some various  suboptimal  options here and we have to make a  judgement about about what the right  thing to do is so I think it's probably  now boiling down to a brexit of this  deal versus a general election and I  would really worry about a general  election when we haven't actually left  the EU I worry about that what that  would do to the Labour Party and the  Labour communities that we represent who  many of whom voted to leave and who were  really angry that we've not actually  respected the outcome of that referendum  so far I mean a couple weeks ago you  signed this letter to the President of  the European Commission along with 18 of  your colleagues and you said if a deal  can be brought back to the Commons in  the coming weeks it avoids a No Deal and  ensures greater certainty during the  UK's departure we believe it serves  Britain's national interest to approve  it I just wonder why if you believe  what's in the letter  you would even consider not backing the  deal because it does take no deal off  the table doesn't it and do you think  that some of your labor labor colleagues  at risk of you know leading us up the  hill a bit hinting that you want to back  a brexit deal but then edging away at  the last minute no we we want there to  be a good deal for Britain and that's  what we were saying in that letter and  actually I think that in part in a very  small part you know maybe those kind of  expressions and and that letter helped  to move on those conversations but it  wasn't a blank check for any deal it  wasn't a blank check for our votes on  anything and as I say we now have over  the coming days the last chance I think  really to shape brexit we've had lots of  chances to shape brexit in the past and  Parliament hasn't been able to come to a  view about the sort of breaks that I was  proposing at that's at that stage you  know a common market solution where we  stayed in the single market but we left  the EU that was rejected by par  as we're all the other options at the  time but now we do have a final chance  because I do think following yesterday  and it's clear from your program this  morning what amber rod was saying in  others that the votes probably are there  now for for a deal so we'll continue to  work as labor colleagues across across  the house and with the labor front bench  to try and shape that deal as best as we  can before the 31st of October it's  interesting you say that you think the  votes are actually there for a deal and  how many of your labor colleagues do you  think they end up supporting it well I  don't I don't know if that will be up to  those labor colleagues but what I would  say is that I found some of the attacks  on on labor colleagues some of the  vilification of Labor colleagues to be  absolutely appalling you know we're  talking about many many labor colleagues  labor constituencies actually not like  mine my seat voted remain but many labor  seats where the vast majority of their  constituents voted to leave and  colleagues are trying to grapple with  these very difficult issues they're  trying to respect the outcome of that  referendum they're trying to respect the  views of their constituents respect the  views of their communities and they're  vilified for doing so and I think that's  something that's got really wrong in in  our politics I mean it shouldn't be an  extreme position should it to say look  we had a referendum the result was  something that I didn't campaign for 